window.PLAYBOOK={"projects": [{"id": "MVE311TA-P1", "title": "Mini Graph-Based STA Engine Validated Against OpenSTA", "course": "MVE311TA", "course_title": "Static Timing Analysis", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 1, "hay": "mve311ta-p1 mini graph-based sta engine validated against opensta static timing analysis mve311ta students use sta as a black box, so slack numbers are never questioned. writing a small engine exposes how arrival times, required times and slew degradation are computed and where tool results can differ. parse a gate-level verilog netlist and a liberty subset (nldm tables with interpolation), build a timing graph, propagate arrival and slew in topological order, run backward required-time propagation, then report setup and hold slack. support create_clock, set_input_delay, set_output_delay. correlate every endpoint against opensta on the same netlist and library. netlist + liberty parser timing graph builder arrival/slew propagation required-time propagation slack report opensta correlation script timing graph algorithms, liberty modelling, delay calculation, tool correlation. units i, ii, iv, v: arcs, nldm delay tables, slew propagation, setup/hold, slack, a subset of sdc"}, {"id": "MVE311TA-P2", "title": "SDC-Driven Timing Closure of an INT8 MAC Array", "course": "MVE311TA", "course_title": "Static Timing Analysis", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 2, "hay": "mve311ta-p2 sdc-driven timing closure of an int8 mac array static timing analysis mve311ta a systolic mac array is the core of every ai accelerator, and its critical path decides the clock. the question is how far constraints, pipelining and retiming can push frequency before area and power rise unacceptably. build a parameterised int8 mac array in verilog, write clock and i/o constraints, synthesise and place it with openroad, then iterate: inspect the worst paths, add pipeline stages, and apply multicycle or false-path exceptions only where they are architecturally justified. document each change and its slack effect. mac array rtl (parametric) sdc constraints yosys synthesis openroad place + sta violation triage pipeline/retime changes timing closure workflow, constraint writing, microarchitecture timing trade-offs. units iv, v: constraints, exceptions, violation fixing; also practical 10 (sdc/sdf generation) of mve212ia"}, {"id": "MVE311TA-P3", "title": "Slack Prediction from Pre-Route Features: A Variation-Aware Timing Study", "course": "MVE311TA", "course_title": "Static Timing Analysis", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 3, "hay": "mve311ta-p3 slack prediction from pre-route features: a variation-aware timing study static timing analysis mve311ta late timing surprises cost design iterations. the hypothesis to test is whether features available before routing (fanout, estimated wire length, cell type, logic depth) can predict post-route slack well enough to guide early optimisation. generate a dataset by running many openroad flows over several open designs with varied constraints and utilisation. extract endpoint features pre-route and slack post-route. train simple models (linear, gradient-boosted trees) and compare to the estimate openroad already gives pre-route. report where models fail (crosstalk, long wires). design/constraint sweeper openroad flow runner feature extractor dataset store model training + eval error analysis data pipeline for eda, ml evaluation discipline, timing physics intuition. units ii, iii, v: parasitics, crosstalk, variability, post-route timing vs earlier estimates"}, {"id": "MVE212IA-P1", "title": "Multiplier Architecture Shootout for INT8 MAC Energy", "course": "MVE212IA", "course_title": "Low Power Digital IC Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 4, "hay": "mve212ia-p1 multiplier architecture shootout for int8 mac energy low power digital ic design mve212ia the multiplier dominates mac energy. which int8 multiplier (array, baugh-wooley, radix-4 booth) is actually lowest energy per operation on realistic data, rather than random inputs? implement array, baugh-wooley and radix-4 booth multipliers with a shared accumulate stage. drive them with random data and with real quantised cnn activations and weights. estimate dynamic power from gate-level switching activity (vcd/saif) with opensta power analysis. three multiplier rtls common mac wrapper data generators (random, cnn) gate-level sim switching-activity power estimate comparison report arithmetic architecture, switching-activity power estimation. unit ii arithmetic blocks; practical 7 (modified booth), practical 8 (adder pre/post synthesis)"}, {"id": "MVE212IA-P2", "title": "Zero-Skipping and Clock-Gated PE Array: Measured Energy on Sparse Activations", "course": "MVE212IA", "course_title": "Low Power Digital IC Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 5, "hay": "mve212ia-p2 zero-skipping and clock-gated pe array: measured energy on sparse activations low power digital ic design mve212ia post-relu activations are often sparse. gating idle processing elements should save dynamic power, but gating logic itself costs area and can hurt timing. build a baseline pe array and a variant with operand isolation, enable-based clock gating (using library icg cells) and zero detection. quantify energy per inference layer using real sparse feature maps from a small quantised cnn. pe (baseline) pe (gated) zero detector array controller activity-based power flow sparsity sweep power-aware microarchitecture, clock gating, power measurement discipline. units iii, v: clock gating, switching-activity reduction, architecture-level power; practical 6 (flip-flops)"}, {"id": "MVE212IA-P3", "title": "Voltage-Parallelism-Frequency Trade-offs for an Accelerator: Calibrated Energy Model", "course": "MVE212IA", "course_title": "Low Power Digital IC Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 6, "hay": "mve212ia-p3 voltage-parallelism-frequency trade-offs for an accelerator: calibrated energy model low power digital ic design mve212ia doubling parallelism and lowering voltage can cut energy per operation, but leakage, memory and interconnect limit the gain. where is the optimum for a mac array under realistic constraints? characterise a mac cell across sky130 corners and supply voltages in ngspice, calibrate an analytical energy/delay model, and combine with a cycle-accurate array simulator to find energy-delay-optimal parallelism per layer. include a power-gating overhead model; state clearly that upf-driven power-gating verification is not fully supported by the open flow. spice characterisation calibrated energy/delay model array simulator optimiser sensitivity analysis quantitative power modelling, spice characterisation, design-space optimisation. unit v: parallel architecture with voltage reduction, power and performance management; unit i power components; practical 3 (process corners)"}, {"id": "MVE313IA-P1", "title": "Boot-to-Driver Firmware Stack from Reset Vector", "course": "MVE313IA", "course_title": "Advanced Embedded System Design", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 7, "hay": "mve313ia-p1 boot-to-driver firmware stack from reset vector advanced embedded system design mve313ia most students call vendor hal functions without understanding startup code, linker scripts and register-level drivers. firmware and driver roles require exactly that understanding. on a cortex-m or risc-v target (hardware or qemu/renode), write the reset handler, vector table, linker script, .data/.bss init, and register-level gpio, uart, systick and i2c drivers with unit tests that run on the emulator. startup + vector table linker script register-level drivers test harness (emulated) application bare-metal programming, linker scripts, driver development. unit iv linker scripts and boot; unit iii memory-mapped i/o; practicals 1-5"}, {"id": "MVE313IA-P2", "title": "RTOS INT8 Inference Runtime with DMA Double-Buffering", "course": "MVE313IA", "course_title": "Advanced Embedded System Design", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 8, "hay": "mve313ia-p2 rtos int8 inference runtime with dma double-buffering advanced embedded system design mve313ia running neural-network inference on a microcontroller is a scheduling, memory and data-movement problem. the question is how much latency and cpu load a naive implementation wastes compared with a dma-overlapped design. run a small int8 cnn (keyword spotting or tiny image classifier) under freertos: sensor-capture task, dma double-buffer, inference task. compare naive c kernels with cmsis-nn style kernels and measure latency, cpu load, and deadline misses. sensor isr + dma double buffer inference task kernel library profiler (cycle counter) rtos design, dma, quantised inference runtime, profiling. unit v rtos, response-time and interrupt-latency evaluation; unit i accelerators/benchmarking; practical rtos 1-4"}, {"id": "MVE313IA-P3", "title": "Secure Boot Chain and Accelerator Driver for a RISC-V Platform", "course": "MVE313IA", "course_title": "Advanced Embedded System Design", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 9, "hay": "mve313ia-p3 secure boot chain and accelerator driver for a risc-v platform advanced embedded system design mve313ia firmware roles at accelerator and soc companies deal with trusted boot and device bring-up. the project asks how a minimal verified boot chain and a memory-mapped accelerator driver can be built and tested. implement stage-0 rom (hash + signature verification of stage-1), stage-1 loader, and an mpu/pmp configuration that protects accelerator registers. provide a driver for a simulated accelerator exposing csrs, dma descriptors and an interrupt. evaluate boot time and attack scenarios (corrupt image, rollback). rom stage 0 signature verification stage 1 loader pmp/mpu config accelerator driver fault/attack tests trusted boot concepts, pmp, driver design. unit iv secure boot, elf loading, linker files; unit i co-processors; unit iii memory-mapped i/o"}, {"id": "MCS214A1-P1", "title": "GEMM Tiling Scheduler: DP and Search for Minimum DRAM Traffic", "course": "MCS214A1", "course_title": "Programming, Data Structures and Algorithms using Python", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 10, "hay": "mcs214a1-p1 gemm tiling scheduler: dp and search for minimum dram traffic programming, data structures and algorithms using python mcs214a1 mapping a matrix multiply onto an accelerator with limited scratchpad is a combinatorial problem. naive tiling wastes dram bandwidth. write a python tool that, given m,n,k, scratchpad size and loop order options, searches tile sizes and loop orders to minimise modelled dram traffic, and outputs a schedule consumable by later accelerator simulators. compare exhaustive search, greedy and dp/branch-and-bound. traffic model search engines schedule exporter validator algorithm design, reuse analysis, tooling. unit v dynamic programming/greedy; unit i algorithm analysis"}, {"id": "MCS214A1-P2", "title": "Netlist Graph Toolkit: Levelization, Critical Path and Partitioning", "course": "MCS214A1", "course_title": "Programming, Data Structures and Algorithms using Python", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 11, "hay": "mcs214a1-p2 netlist graph toolkit: levelization, critical path and partitioning programming, data structures and algorithms using python mcs214a1 netlists are graphs. engineers who can analyse them programmatically can build custom checks that tools do not provide. parse yosys json netlists to a graph, implement levelization, longest-path (critical path) analysis, fanout statistics, loop detection, and a kernighan-lin partitioner. run on real open-source designs and visualise results. yosys json parser graph core algorithms reports and plots graph algorithms on eda data. unit iv graph algorithms, shortest paths, spanning trees, dfs/bfs; unit ii hashing; unit iv heaps"}, {"id": "MCS214A1-P3", "title": "Memory Trace Cache Simulator with Tile-Aware Replacement Policies", "course": "MCS214A1", "course_title": "Programming, Data Structures and Algorithms using Python", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 12, "hay": "mcs214a1-p3 memory trace cache simulator with tile-aware replacement policies programming, data structures and algorithms using python mcs214a1 cache replacement policies are tuned for general workloads, but accelerator access patterns are loop-nest structured. can a policy that knows the tile schedule beat lru? build a trace-driven cache simulator with lru, lfu, fifo, belady-opt and a custom tile-aware policy fed by the schedule from the gemm scheduler. generate traces from gemm and convolution loop nests and evaluate hit rates and dram traffic. trace generator cache model policies evaluator cache architecture, data structures, evaluation methodology. unit ii hashing/queues; unit iv heaps; unit v algorithm design; unit i analysis"}, {"id": "MDC214A2-P1", "title": "8x8 2D-DCT and Quantiser Core in RTL (JPEG Datapath)", "course": "MDC214A2", "course_title": "Multimedia Communications", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 13, "hay": "mdc214a2-p1 8x8 2d-dct and quantiser core in rtl (jpeg datapath) multimedia communications mdc214a2 a dct/quantiser pipeline is a compact example of a fixed-point streaming datapath. the design question is throughput, precision and area with defined psnr. implement row-column 2d dct with fixed-point coefficients and a transpose buffer, plus quantisation. verify against a floating-point reference and measure psnr on standard test images. 1d dct transpose buffer quantiser axi-stream wrapper reference model fixed-point datapath design, streaming interfaces. unit ii jpeg compression pipeline; unit i representation"}, {"id": "MDC214A2-P2", "title": "CUDA Block-Matching Motion Estimation: Optimisation Ladder", "course": "MDC214A2", "course_title": "Multimedia Communications", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 14, "hay": "mdc214a2-p2 cuda block-matching motion estimation: optimisation ladder multimedia communications mdc214a2 sad-based motion search is regular but memory-bound. the project shows how tiling, shared memory and coalescing change performance and how to prove it with profiling. implement full-search sad for 16x16 blocks: cpu baseline, naive cuda, shared-memory tiled, and vectorised versions. profile with nsight compute, build a roofline plot, and compare with opencv if available. cpu baseline naive kernel tiled kernel vectorised kernel profiler analysis cuda optimisation, profiling, roofline thinking. unit iii video compression (motion estimation concepts, h.26x/mpeg families)"}, {"id": "MDC214A2-P3", "title": "Feature-Map Compression to Cut Accelerator DRAM Bandwidth", "course": "MDC214A2", "course_title": "Multimedia Communications", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 15, "hay": "mdc214a2-p3 feature-map compression to cut accelerator dram bandwidth multimedia communications mdc214a2 dram bandwidth often limits accelerators. activations are sparse and compressible; the question is the compression ratio versus hardware cost and accuracy loss. capture activations from a small cnn, evaluate zero-run-length, bitmask and quantised-transform codecs, model decoder/encoder hardware cost, and compute net bandwidth saving using the gemm scheduler. related published work exists on activation compression, so the contribution is an honest comparative study. activation capture codec library hardware cost model bandwidth model accuracy check memory-bandwidth reasoning, compression design. unit ii/iii compression principles (transform, quantisation, entropy coding) applied to activations"}, {"id": "MPE214A3-P1", "title": "Battery SoC Estimation: EKF in Fixed-Point C on an MCU", "course": "MPE214A3", "course_title": "Electric and Hybrid Vehicles", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 16, "hay": "mpe214a3-p1 battery soc estimation: ekf in fixed-point c on an mcu electric and hybrid vehicles mpe214a3 coulomb counting drifts. an extended kalman filter fixes it but must run in real time on a small mcu in fixed point. build an equivalent-circuit battery model, validate on a public cell dataset, implement coulomb counting and an ekf in float then fixed point, and run on an mcu or emulator with timing measured. cell model ekf fixed-point conversion mcu harness evaluation state estimation, fixed-point embedded implementation. unit iii battery energy storage and management; unit i performance models"}, {"id": "MPE214A3-P2", "title": "FOC Motor-Control Pipeline in RTL (Clarke, Park, PI, SVPWM)", "course": "MPE214A3", "course_title": "Electric and Hybrid Vehicles", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 17, "hay": "mpe214a3-p2 foc motor-control pipeline in rtl (clarke, park, pi, svpwm) electric and hybrid vehicles mpe214a3 field-oriented control needs deterministic microsecond loops. hardware pipelines achieve fixed latency that software on a busy mcu cannot guarantee. implement clarke, park, pi controllers and svpwm in fixed-point verilog with a python or verilator motor-plant model in the loop. measure loop latency, quantisation effects and stability. adc interface model clarke/park pi svpwm plant model hil harness fixed-point control hardware, co-simulation. unit ii motor drive control (induction, pm motors); unit v converters"}, {"id": "MPE214A3-P3", "title": "Thermal- and Power-Aware Governor for an Automotive Edge AI Accelerator", "course": "MPE214A3", "course_title": "Electric and Hybrid Vehicles", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 18, "hay": "mpe214a3-p3 thermal- and power-aware governor for an automotive edge ai accelerator electric and hybrid vehicles mpe214a3 vehicle ecus have strict power and thermal limits. a runtime governor must trade inference throughput against temperature. build an rc thermal model and power model for an accelerator (calibrated from your low-power study), implement governor policies (threshold, pid, predictive), and evaluate under drive-cycle workloads in simulation. state clearly it is simulation. workload generator power model thermal rc model governor policies evaluation systems modelling, control policy. unit iii energy management strategies; unit i power-source and performance modelling"}, {"id": "MVE214A4-P1", "title": "Parameterised INT8 MAC Array and GEMM Engine on FPGA", "course": "MVE214A4", "course_title": "Digital System Design with FPGA", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 19, "hay": "mve214a4-p1 parameterised int8 mac array and gemm engine on fpga digital system design with fpga mve214a4 fpga dsp and bram mapping decide performance. students rarely examine how rtl maps to resources. design a weight-stationary 4x4 to 16x16 mac array with bram tiles and a small controller; verify against numpy; synthesise and record resource use and fmax. pe array bram tile buffers fsm controller testbench + numpy golden fpga mapping, datapath design. units ii, iii, v: modelling, synthesis, datapath and controller design"}, {"id": "MVE214A4-P2", "title": "Streaming 3x3 Convolution Engine with Line Buffers", "course": "MVE214A4", "course_title": "Digital System Design with FPGA", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 20, "hay": "mve214a4-p2 streaming 3x3 convolution engine with line buffers digital system design with fpga mve214a4 streaming convolution minimises memory traffic by reuse via line buffers. correct boundary handling is subtle. build a pixel-stream 3x3 conv with bram line buffers, int8 weights, padding modes, backpressure. verify against opencv/numpy on images; measure throughput. line buffers window generator mac tree backpressure fsm reference streaming datapaths, handshakes. units iii, iv, v: synthesis, fpga fabric, filters and signal processors"}, {"id": "MVE214A4-P3", "title": "RV32I 5-Stage Core from Scratch with riscv-formal Checks", "course": "MVE214A4", "course_title": "Digital System Design with FPGA", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 21, "hay": "mve214a4-p3 rv32i 5-stage core from scratch with riscv-formal checks digital system design with fpga mve214a4 designing a pipelined cpu teaches hazards, forwarding, and verification at depth. the portfolio value is a verified core you control and can extend with custom instructions. implement rv32i with pipeline, forwarding, hazard detection, csr basics, and a memory interface; verify with the risc-v compliance tests and riscv-formal; run on fpga or simulation executing c programs. fetch decode execute memory writeback hazard unit formal harness cpu microarchitecture, formal verification. unit v processor design and system development; units i-iii for hdl modelling"}, {"id": "MVE415DL-P1", "title": "On-Chip Variation Monte-Carlo Study: Guardband Advisor for Designers", "course": "MVE415DL", "course_title": "Design Thinking Lab", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 22, "hay": "mve415dl-p1 on-chip variation monte-carlo study: guardband advisor for designers design thinking lab mve415dl designers guardband timing for variation but often without quantitative feedback. the user need: understand how much margin a path needs. interview 5+ designers/peers, define the problem, then prototype a monte-carlo ring-oscillator and critical-path study in ngspice (sky130 mismatch models) and a small tool that recommends margin vs path depth. user research problem statement ngspice monte-carlo advisor script user test variation physics, user-centred process. theme: on-chip variation analysis; stages empathize through test"}, {"id": "MVE415DL-P2", "title": "Designer-Experience Dashboard for OpenROAD Run Reports", "course": "MVE415DL", "course_title": "Design Thinking Lab", "sem": 1, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 23, "hay": "mve415dl-p2 designer-experience dashboard for openroad run reports design thinking lab mve415dl reading flow logs is slow. a better view of timing, power and area across runs speeds iteration. parse openroad reports from many runs, build a web dashboard comparing ppa over runs, and run usability tests measuring time-to-find-answer versus raw logs. parser data store web ui usability protocol tooling, ux evaluation. theme: system design; stages prototype and test"}, {"id": "MVE415DL-P3", "title": "Timing-Speculation (Razor-Style) Resilience Study for a MAC Array", "course": "MVE415DL", "course_title": "Design Thinking Lab", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 24, "hay": "mve415dl-p3 timing-speculation (razor-style) resilience study for a mac array design thinking lab mve415dl variation forces guardbands. timing speculation with error detection can run closer to the edge, but errors affect neural-network accuracy. model timing errors in a mac array using a variation-aware delay distribution; inject bit errors into a quantised network and measure accuracy vs frequency; compare detect-and-replay vs ignore. the idea is based on published razor work (ernst et al., micro 2003); your contribution is the accelerator-level study. delay distribution error injector nn evaluation replay cost model cross-layer modelling. theme: variation; stage ideate/prototype; ties to mve311ta and mve212ia"}, {"id": "HSS116EL-P1", "title": "Architecture Specification for an Accelerator (Design-Spec Document)", "course": "HSS116EL", "course_title": "Technical English", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "2-3", "w0": 2, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 25, "hay": "hss116el-p1 architecture specification for an accelerator (design-spec document) technical english hss116el weak specifications cause rework. the project writes the kind of spec used inside chip teams. write a 12-15 page architecture spec for the mac-array accelerator: goals, interfaces, register map, timing diagrams, verification plan. peer review with a checklist. template draft peer review revision technical writing. unit i business/technical documents; unit ii recommendations; unit iii audience needs"}, {"id": "HSS116EL-P2", "title": "Evidence-Based White Paper: INT8 vs FP16 Inference Trade-offs", "course": "HSS116EL", "course_title": "Technical English", "sem": 1, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 26, "hay": "hss116el-p2 evidence-based white paper: int8 vs fp16 inference trade-offs technical english hss116el marketing claims about precision are often misleading. a white paper must use sources and data honestly. using your own measurements (quantisation experiments) and vetted sources, write a 10-page white paper with recommendations and limits; include a source-quality table. literature data analysis draft review evidence-based communication. unit ii interpreting data, ethical persuasion, recommendations; unit iii trustworthy sources"}, {"id": "HSS116EL-P3", "title": "Conference-Style Paper and Talk Replicating a Published Roofline Study", "course": "HSS116EL", "course_title": "Technical English", "sem": 1, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-6", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-1.html", "ord": 27, "hay": "hss116el-p3 conference-style paper and talk replicating a published roofline study technical english hss116el reproducing a published analysis teaches research communication and honest comparison. replicate a roofline analysis (williams et al., cacm 2009) for gemm on your cpu/gpu, write an ieee-format 4-page paper with reproducibility artefacts, and give a 10-minute talk; also peer-review two papers. replication paper talk peer review research communication. unit iii genres, standards vs white papers; unit ii proposals; presentation skills"}, {"id": "MVE321IA-P1", "title": "Two-Stage Miller Op-Amp: Spec to Post-Layout in sky130", "course": "MVE321IA", "course_title": "Analog IC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 28, "hay": "mve321ia-p1 two-stage miller op-amp: spec to post-layout in sky130 analog ic design mve321ia hand-calculated sizing rarely survives simulation, and layout parasitics erode phase margin. the project closes the loop from specification to post-layout. size a two-stage miller-compensated op-amp from specs (gain, gbw, pm, power), simulate dc/ac/transient/corners in ngspice, draw layout in magic or klayout with matching care, extract parasitics, and compare pre- and post-layout. spec sheet hand sizing (gm/id) ngspice testbenches layout pex corner report analog design flow, compensation, layout parasitics. units ii, iii: op-amp topologies, compensation; practicals 7, 9, 10"}, {"id": "MVE321IA-P2", "title": "Bandgap Reference and LDO for an Accelerator Rail", "course": "MVE321IA", "course_title": "Analog IC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 29, "hay": "mve321ia-p2 bandgap reference and ldo for an accelerator rail analog ic design mve321ia a mac array draws pulsed current. the regulator must keep droop within the digital timing budget while remaining stable. design a bandgap and a pmos ldo; drive the ldo with a load-current profile derived from the switching activity of your mac array (from mve212ia); evaluate psrr, load transient, loop stability across load. bandgap error amp pass device load profile stability analysis analog power delivery, mixed-domain analysis. unit iv bandgap, ldo; unit iii stability; practical 8"}, {"id": "MVE321IA-P3", "title": "Power-Delivery Droop Co-Analysis: From RTL Activity to Clock-Stretch Mitigation", "course": "MVE321IA", "course_title": "Analog IC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 30, "hay": "mve321ia-p3 power-delivery droop co-analysis: from rtl activity to clock-stretch mitigation analog ic design mve321ia large di/dt events in accelerators cause voltage droop and timing failures. the question is how much droop a given pdn and workload produce and how to mitigate it. extract per-cycle current estimates from rtl activity, model the pdn (r, l, c) in ngspice, compute droop for workloads, and evaluate mitigations: staggered start, decap sizing, and a clock-stretch behaviour model. simulation-level study; related published work on di/dt management exists. activity extraction pdn model droop simulation mitigation models trade-off analysis power integrity concepts. unit iii stability/feedback concepts; unit iv regulators; connection to mve212ia activity data"}, {"id": "MVE322IA-P1", "title": "Reproducible RTL-to-GDS of a MAC Array with OpenLane", "course": "MVE322IA", "course_title": "Physical Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 31, "hay": "mve322ia-p1 reproducible rtl-to-gds of a mac array with openlane physical design mve322ia a scripted, reproducible physical flow is the base for all ppa claims. run an end-to-end open flow (synthesis, floorplan, placement, cts, routing, signoff checks) on a mac array, with a one-command makefile and archived reports. rtl config flow reports signoff checks physical design flow. units ii, iii, v; practicals 3-4, 6, 8-9"}, {"id": "MVE322IA-P2", "title": "Systolic Array Physical-Design Exploration: Aspect Ratio, Macros and Power Grid", "course": "MVE322IA", "course_title": "Physical Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 32, "hay": "mve322ia-p2 systolic array physical-design exploration: aspect ratio, macros and power grid physical design mve322ia regular arrays are wire-dominated. floorplan choices change wirelength, congestion and timing. sweep array size, aspect ratio, utilisation, pdn pitch, and sram macro placement for a 16x16 array with scratchpads; report ppa pareto and ir drop. parameter sweeper flow runner metrics db pareto plots floorplanning, ppa analysis. units ii, iii: macro placement, power planning, clock planning, ir drop"}, {"id": "MVE322IA-P3", "title": "Dataflow-Aware Placement: Regular vs Flat Placement for PE Arrays", "course": "MVE322IA", "course_title": "Physical Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 33, "hay": "mve322ia-p3 dataflow-aware placement: regular vs flat placement for pe arrays physical design mve322ia automatic placers ignore array regularity. would placement hints that preserve the pe grid improve wirelength and timing? compare the default placer with region constraints and structured placement hints on pe arrays of several sizes; measure hpwl, timing, power, clock skew. be explicit that this is a controlled experiment on open tools. array generator placement variants metrics statistical analysis experimental method in eda. units ii, iii: global/detail placement, clock planning; ties to mve331ta"}, {"id": "MVE323B1-P1", "title": "Constrained-Random Class-Based Testbench for an AXI-Stream FIFO", "course": "MVE323B1", "course_title": "SystemVerilog for Design and Verification", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 34, "hay": "mve323b1-p1 constrained-random class-based testbench for an axi-stream fifo systemverilog for design and verification mve323b1 directed tests miss corner cases. a class-based random testbench with scoreboard and coverage shows verification fundamentals. build generator, driver, monitor, scoreboard and coverage groups for an axi-stream fifo with sva protocol checks. seed bugs to confirm the bench catches them. interface generator driver monitor scoreboard coverage + sva sv verification methodology. units ii, iii, iv: classes, randomisation, interfaces, coverage"}, {"id": "MVE323B1-P2", "title": "AXI4-Lite / AXI-Stream Verification IP and Protocol Assertions for Accelerator CSRs", "course": "MVE323B1", "course_title": "SystemVerilog for Design and Verification", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 35, "hay": "mve323b1-p2 axi4-lite / axi-stream verification ip and protocol assertions for accelerator csrs systemverilog for design and verification mve323b1 soc integration bugs hide at interfaces. a reusable bus agent with checkers is core industrial verification work. build axi4-lite master/slave agents, a register model, sva for protocol rules (valid/ready stability, response rules), and run against your accelerator csr block; report coverage closure. axi-lite agent register model sva checker test sequences coverage bus protocols, vip design. units iii, iv, v: interfaces, assertions, uvm-style agents"}, {"id": "MVE323B1-P3", "title": "Mutation-Tested Verification of a Systolic GEMM Engine", "course": "MVE323B1", "course_title": "SystemVerilog for Design and Verification", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 36, "hay": "mve323b1-p3 mutation-tested verification of a systolic gemm engine systemverilog for design and verification mve323b1 coverage does not prove a testbench finds bugs. mutation analysis estimates bug-finding power. build a verification environment for the gemm engine with a reference model; automatically inject mutants into the rtl; measure kill rate versus coverage and compare constrained-random against directed and formal checks. reference model environment mutator kill-rate analysis verification quality metrics. units iv, v: coverage and assertions, uvm; methodology evaluation"}, {"id": "MVE323B2-P1", "title": "RF Cascade and Matching-Network Design Tool", "course": "MVE323B2", "course_title": "RFIC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 37, "hay": "mve323b2-p1 rf cascade and matching-network design tool rfic design mve323b2 hand calculation of cascades and l-match networks is error-prone. a validated tool builds intuition. python tool using scikit-rf for cascaded nf/iip3/gain, l and pi matching, and smith-chart plots, validated against ngspice ac analysis of the same networks. cascade calculator matching designer ngspice validation rf system analysis. units i, iii: noise figure, iip3, matching networks"}, {"id": "MVE323B2-P2", "title": "Ring/LC Oscillator Phase-Noise Study", "course": "MVE323B2", "course_title": "RFIC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 38, "hay": "mve323b2-p2 ring/lc oscillator phase-noise study rfic design mve323b2 oscillator noise limits clock quality. ring oscillators are common in digital socs; their jitter matters for timing. design a ring oscillator and an lc oscillator in ngspice, estimate phase noise via transient noise and fft, compare against analytical models (leeson/hajimiri) and relate jitter to an sta uncertainty budget. ring oscillator lc oscillator noise sim jitter conversion oscillator/jitter analysis. unit iv oscillators; unit v pll; unit i noise"}, {"id": "MVE323B2-P3", "title": "Digital Down-Converter RTL: CIC, FIR and I/Q Correction", "course": "MVE323B2", "course_title": "RFIC Design", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-9", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 39, "hay": "mve323b2-p3 digital down-converter rtl: cic, fir and i/q correction rfic design mve323b2 modern receivers shift work into digital. a ddc is a streaming-dsp datapath with real throughput and precision trade-offs. implement nco, mixer, cic decimator, compensating fir and i/q imbalance correction in rtl; verify in python; evaluate snr, resource use, and bit-width choices. nco mixer cic fir iq correction python model dsp datapath design. units ii, v: receiver architectures (homodyne, image rejection), pll-based lo concepts"}, {"id": "MVE323B3-P1", "title": "1D Drift-Diffusion Solver for PN Junctions", "course": "MVE323B3", "course_title": "Semiconductor Device Modelling", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 40, "hay": "mve323b3-p1 1d drift-diffusion solver for pn junctions semiconductor device modelling mve323b3 black-box device simulators hide numerical issues. writing a solver builds depth. implement a 1d poisson + drift-diffusion solver (scharfetter-gummel) in python, validate against the ideal diode equation and depletion approximations, and explore doping and recombination effects. mesh poisson continuity solver validation numerical device modelling. units i, ii: poisson, drift-diffusion, recombination, i-v"}, {"id": "MVE323B3-P2", "title": "EKV-Style Compact Model Extraction from sky130 SPICE Sweeps", "course": "MVE323B3", "course_title": "Semiconductor Device Modelling", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 41, "hay": "mve323b3-p2 ekv-style compact model extraction from sky130 spice sweeps semiconductor device modelling mve323b3 compact models drive circuit and architecture studies. a fitted model can generate fast delay/energy look-up tables. extract parameters of an ekv-like model from ngspice sweeps of sky130 devices, compare fit quality, and build gm/id tables used by the analog and low-power projects. sweep generator fitting validation lut export compact-model fitting. unit iv mos: square-law, bulk-charge, small-signal; model concepts"}, {"id": "MVE323B3-P3", "title": "Cross-Layer Variability: Device Mismatch to Network Accuracy", "course": "MVE323B3", "course_title": "Semiconductor Device Modelling", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 42, "hay": "mve323b3-p3 cross-layer variability: device mismatch to network accuracy semiconductor device modelling mve323b3 device variation shifts timing and analog values. how much matters for neural-network accuracy? take vth/mismatch distributions from monte-carlo spice, map to mac delay error or analog noise, inject in quantised network inference, and quantify accuracy vs variation level. mc spice error mapping inference injection mitigation study cross-layer reasoning. units iv, v: mos behaviour and advanced device effects"}, {"id": "MVE323B4-P1", "title": "CAN Stack and Response-Time Analysis", "course": "MVE323B4", "course_title": "Automotive Embedded Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 43, "hay": "mve323b4-p1 can stack and response-time analysis automotive embedded systems mve323b4 message latency on can depends on priorities and load. analysis predicts worst-case response times. implement a can driver and message scheduler on linux vcan or renode, perform response-time analysis, and compare analytic bounds with measured latencies. can driver scheduler analyser measurement automotive networking. unit v automotive protocols and network simulation (can is the common baseline alongside the listed lin, most, flexray)"}, {"id": "MVE323B4-P2", "title": "AUTOSAR-Style Layered ECU Software with MISRA-C Checks", "course": "MVE323B4", "course_title": "Automotive Embedded Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 44, "hay": "mve323b4-p2 autosar-style layered ecu software with misra-c checks automotive embedded systems mve323b4 automotive firmware follows layered architectures and coding rules. wcet and deadline analysis matter. build an rtos-based ecu software with layers (mcal, services, application) running a small lane-detection-like inference task, check misra-c compliance with a static analyser, measure deadline misses and wcet. mcal rtos app task static analysis timing embedded safety coding. unit v autosar architecture; unit iv adas applications; embedded software design (misra-c appears in mve313ia)"}, {"id": "MVE323B4-P3", "title": "Soft-Error Fault Injection and Mitigation for a Systolic Array (ISO 26262 Concepts)", "course": "MVE323B4", "course_title": "Automotive Embedded Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 45, "hay": "mve323b4-p3 soft-error fault injection and mitigation for a systolic array (iso 26262 concepts) automotive embedded systems mve323b4 automotive chips need quantified resilience. soft errors in mac arrays can silently corrupt results. inject bit flips into registers of the systolic array rtl via simulation; classify outcomes (masked, sdc, due); evaluate parity, ecc and selective tmr for area overhead versus coverage. fault injector outcome classifier mitigation rtl area model safety analysis, fault injection. units ii, iv, v: sensors, adas and diagnostics. note: functional safety is not a listed unit here (it appears in mve313ia); this project adds depth beyond the syllabus"}, {"id": "MCS324C1-P1", "title": "Quantisation Study: PTQ vs QAT from FP32 to INT4 with RTL Weight Export", "course": "MCS324C1", "course_title": "Computer Vision with DL", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 46, "hay": "mcs324c1-p1 quantisation study: ptq vs qat from fp32 to int4 with rtl weight export computer vision with dl mcs324c1 accelerator design needs accuracy-vs-bit-width data from real networks. train a small cnn, apply ptq and qat at int8/int6/int4, report accuracy and layerwise sensitivity, and export weights and activation scales as hex for rtl tests. training ptq qat export tool quantisation methods. unit iv cnn classification"}, {"id": "MCS324C1-P2", "title": "GPU-Accelerated Feature Pipeline: Gaussian, Sobel and Harris in CUDA", "course": "MCS324C1", "course_title": "Computer Vision with DL", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 47, "hay": "mcs324c1-p2 gpu-accelerated feature pipeline: gaussian, sobel and harris in cuda computer vision with dl mcs324c1 image filters are bandwidth-bound; kernel structure determines throughput. write cuda kernels for separable gaussian, sobel and harris; optimise with shared-memory tiles and constant memory; compare to opencv cpu and (if available) opencv cuda. kernels benchmark harness profiler analysis cuda optimisation. units ii, iii: filtering, convolution, edges, corners"}, {"id": "MCS324C1-P3", "title": "Layer-Fused Conv Accelerator Demo: End-to-End Tiny CNN in RTL", "course": "MCS324C1", "course_title": "Computer Vision with DL", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "10-14", "w0": 10, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 48, "hay": "mcs324c1-p3 layer-fused conv accelerator demo: end-to-end tiny cnn in rtl computer vision with dl mcs324c1 executing layer by layer spills activations to dram. layer fusion keeps them on-chip, but needs buffering and scheduling. implement a small cnn (for example mnist/cifar-scale) in rtl with and without fusion across two layers; measure memory traffic and cycles; verify against quantised pytorch. conv engine buffers fusion controller sw golden dataflow/fusion design. unit iv cnns; unit v detection/recognition workloads"}, {"id": "MDC324C2-P1", "title": "Routing Protocol Simulator: AODV vs DSDV vs DSR with Energy Metrics", "course": "MDC324C2", "course_title": "Adhoc Networks", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 49, "hay": "mdc324c2-p1 routing protocol simulator: aodv vs dsdv vs dsr with energy metrics adhoc networks mdc324c2 protocol choice affects delivery, overhead and energy. use ns-3 (or simpy) to compare aodv, dsdv and dsr across mobility and load; report pdr, delay, overhead and energy. scenario generator simulator metrics network simulation. unit iii routing; unit v energy"}, {"id": "MDC324C2-P2", "title": "Mesh NoC Routing Algorithm Study: XY vs Odd-Even vs Adaptive", "course": "MDC324C2", "course_title": "Adhoc Networks", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 50, "hay": "mdc324c2-p2 mesh noc routing algorithm study: xy vs odd-even vs adaptive adhoc networks mdc324c2 noc routing determines latency, throughput and deadlock freedom in multi-core socs. use booksim 2 or a custom python simulator to evaluate routing algorithms on 4x4 to 8x8 meshes with traffic patterns; produce latency-throughput curves and deadlock analysis. traffic generators routers (model) metrics noc routing. units ii, iii, v: mac contention analogies, routing, qos (applied to on-chip networks)"}, {"id": "MDC324C2-P3", "title": "CSMA/CA Backoff MAC Controller in RTL with SVA", "course": "MDC324C2", "course_title": "Adhoc Networks", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 51, "hay": "mdc324c2-p3 csma/ca backoff mac controller in rtl with sva adhoc networks mdc324c2 hardware mac controllers must meet tight timing. verification is protocol-heavy. implement a csma/ca mac (carrier sense, binary exponential backoff, ack timeout) in rtl, verify with sva and a python network model co-simulated. carrier sense backoff tx fsm sva checker co-sim model protocol hardware. unit ii mac protocols, contention-based with reservation"}, {"id": "MPE324C3-P1", "title": "Fuzzy-PI Speed Controller: Simulation to Fixed-Point MCU Code", "course": "MPE324C3", "course_title": "Intelligent Control Techniques in Electrical Drives", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 52, "hay": "mpe324c3-p1 fuzzy-pi speed controller: simulation to fixed-point mcu code intelligent control techniques in electrical drives mpe324c3 fuzzy controllers must be implemented efficiently on mcus. design a mamdani flc for dc motor speed, compare with pid in simulation, and implement the inference in fixed-point c with lookup tables; measure cycles. plant model flc fixed-point port evaluation controller implementation. units i, ii: fuzzy sets, flc design"}, {"id": "MPE324C3-P2", "title": "MLP Controller on FPGA: Microsecond Closed-Loop Neural Inference", "course": "MPE324C3", "course_title": "Intelligent Control Techniques in Electrical Drives", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 53, "hay": "mpe324c3-p2 mlp controller on fpga: microsecond closed-loop neural inference intelligent control techniques in electrical drives mpe324c3 neural controllers need deterministic low-latency inference. train an mlp controller, quantise, implement in rtl pipeline, close loop with a plant model, and measure latency and control quality. mlp rtl plant co-sim quantisation low-latency inference hardware. units iii, iv: neural network control"}, {"id": "MPE324C3-P3", "title": "Parallel GA/PSO Controller Tuning on GPU", "course": "MPE324C3", "course_title": "Intelligent Control Techniques in Electrical Drives", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 18, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 54, "hay": "mpe324c3-p3 parallel ga/pso controller tuning on gpu intelligent control techniques in electrical drives mpe324c3 tuning requires evaluating many candidate controllers; the evaluations are independent. implement ga and pso to tune controllers over a simulated plant, with cuda kernels evaluating populations in parallel; measure speedup and convergence. plant simulator kernel ga/pso benchmark parallel algorithms. unit v: ga, pso, hybrid algorithms"}, {"id": "MVE324C4-P1", "title": "Diffusion and Deal-Grove Oxidation Simulators", "course": "MVE324C4", "course_title": "Semiconductor Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 55, "hay": "mve324c4-p1 diffusion and deal-grove oxidation simulators semiconductor manufacturing mve324c4 process simulators hide assumptions; building simple ones clarifies them. implement 1d diffusion (finite difference) and deal-grove oxidation in python; validate against analytical solutions; explore dopant profiles. fd solver deal-grove validation process modelling. units ii, iii: fick's law, oxidation model"}, {"id": "MVE324C4-P2", "title": "Die Cost and Yield Model: Monolithic vs Chiplet Accelerator", "course": "MVE324C4", "course_title": "Semiconductor Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 56, "hay": "mve324c4-p2 die cost and yield model: monolithic vs chiplet accelerator semiconductor manufacturing mve324c4 die size drives cost through yield and wafer packing. architects need such models. build a parametric die-per-wafer and yield (poisson/negative binomial) cost model, compare monolithic accelerator with chiplet splits including packaging overhead as explicit assumptions. wafer packing yield models cost model sensitivity cost/yield reasoning. unit v statistics and process control (yield statistics); unit i wafers"}, {"id": "MVE324C4-P3", "title": "Virtual-Wafer Variation to Speed Binning of Accelerator Dies", "course": "MVE324C4", "course_title": "Semiconductor Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 57, "hay": "mve324c4-p3 virtual-wafer variation to speed binning of accelerator dies semiconductor manufacturing mve324c4 parametric yield depends on spatial and random variation. speed binning decides revenue. generate virtual wafers with systematic and random parameter variation, map to path delay and leakage using your spice/sta models, simulate speed bins, and analyse with control charts. wafer generator delay/leakage mapping binning spc charts statistical process thinking. units iv, v: wafer mapping, resistivity, spc, nested variance"}, {"id": "MBT325DA-P1", "title": "Evolutionary Design-Space Exploration of Accelerator Configurations", "course": "MBT325DA", "course_title": "Nature Impelled Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 58, "hay": "mbt325da-p1 evolutionary design-space exploration of accelerator configurations nature impelled engineering mbt325da accelerator parameters (pe count, sram size, dataflow) interact non-linearly. bio-inspired search can explore them efficiently. build an analytical accelerator model (latency, energy, area) and search it with ga, pso and random search; compare convergence and pareto quality. analytical model ga pso random baseline pareto analysis optimisation, dse. unit v: genetic algorithms, evolutionary computing, swarm intelligence"}, {"id": "MBT325DA-P2", "title": "Spiking LIF Neuron Array in RTL: Event-Driven vs Dense Energy", "course": "MBT325DA", "course_title": "Nature Impelled Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 59, "hay": "mbt325da-p2 spiking lif neuron array in rtl: event-driven vs dense energy nature impelled engineering mbt325da spiking networks promise energy savings through sparsity. the claim must be tested against a dense baseline on the same task. implement leaky-integrate-and-fire neuron arrays with event queues, run a small classification task, and compare synaptic operations and estimated energy against a quantised dense network. lif neuron event queue synapse memory dense baseline neuromorphic architecture. unit v: neural networks, bio-inspired computing"}, {"id": "MBT325DA-P3", "title": "Self-Repairing PE Array: Spare-Row/Column Reconfiguration", "course": "MBT325DA", "course_title": "Nature Impelled Engineering", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 60, "hay": "mbt325da-p3 self-repairing pe array: spare-row/column reconfiguration nature impelled engineering mbt325da manufacturing defects disable pes. reconfiguration can salvage dies, but remap logic costs area. design spare-row/column remapping for a systolic array with fault maps, implement rtl remap and a python yield analysis (defect density sweep). fault map remap logic yield analysis fault tolerance. units i, iv, v: biological robustness and self-organisation applied to hardware"}, {"id": "MBT325DB-P1", "title": "Fixed-Point Pan-Tompkins QRS Detector on MCU with PhysioNet Data", "course": "MBT325DB", "course_title": "Clinical Data Management", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 61, "hay": "mbt325db-p1 fixed-point pan-tompkins qrs detector on mcu with physionet data clinical data management mbt325db wearables need low-power signal processing on constrained devices. implement pan-tompkins qrs detection in floating point, then fixed-point c, validate on public physionet ecg data (for example mit-bih), report sensitivity and positive predictivity, and measure cycles on an mcu model. filter chain detection logic fixed-point port evaluation signal processing on embedded. unit i data types, analytics; unit v privacy awareness"}, {"id": "MBT325DB-P2", "title": "Hardware-Experiment Data Management Pipeline with Validation and Audit Trail", "course": "MBT325DB", "course_title": "Clinical Data Management", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 62, "hay": "mbt325db-p2 hardware-experiment data management pipeline with validation and audit trail clinical data management mbt325db ppa results across many runs become unreliable without schema, validation and provenance, much like clinical data quality control. design a schema and validation rules for eda/benchmark results, ingest all your project runs with provenance (tool versions, configs, git hash), detect anomalies, and generate audit reports. schema ingestion validation rules provenance reports data quality engineering. units i, iii, v: data cleaning, standards, integrity, auditability"}, {"id": "MBT325DB-P3", "title": "Wearable ECG Arrhythmia Classifier Accelerator (INT8 1D-CNN)", "course": "MBT325DB", "course_title": "Clinical Data Management", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "9-12", "w0": 9, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 63, "hay": "mbt325db-p3 wearable ecg arrhythmia classifier accelerator (int8 1d-cnn) clinical data management mbt325db ultra-low-power inference for wearables needs specialised hardware; fairness and data issues matter. train a small 1d-cnn on public ecg data, quantise to int8, implement a streaming rtl accelerator, estimate energy per heartbeat classification, and discuss bias/ethics limits. 1d-cnn quantisation rtl energy estimate domain accelerator. units i, v: healthcare analytics, ai and ml, ethics and bias"}, {"id": "MCN325DC-P1", "title": "Firmware Image Forensics Toolkit with Chain-of-Custody Logging", "course": "MCN325DC", "course_title": "Cyber Forensics and Cyber Laws", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 64, "hay": "mcn325dc-p1 firmware image forensics toolkit with chain-of-custody logging cyber forensics and cyber laws mcn325dc firmware analysis requires evidence integrity and documentation, and builds embedded-systems understanding. build a toolkit that hashes, extracts and inventories sections of open firmware images (use your own builds or open-source images), logging every action in a tamper-evident chain-of-custody log. hash tool extractor inventory custody log firmware layout, integrity. units ii, iii: evidence handling, hashing and imaging, reporting"}, {"id": "MCN325DC-P2", "title": "Side-Channel Leakage Assessment (TVLA) of an RTL Datapath", "course": "MCN325DC", "course_title": "Cyber Forensics and Cyber Laws", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 65, "hay": "mcn325dc-p2 side-channel leakage assessment (tvla) of an rtl datapath cyber forensics and cyber laws mcn325dc hardware leaks through power. defenders assess leakage on their own designs before fabrication. generate simulated power traces from switching activity of your own aes or mac rtl, apply tvla (t-test) leakage assessment, add a simple masking countermeasure, and quantify the effect. defensive evaluation of your own design only. trace generator tvla countermeasure hardware security assessment. units iii, v: security architecture, risk management"}, {"id": "MCN325DC-P3", "title": "Tamper-Evident Hardware Event Logger with SHA-256 Hash Chain", "course": "MCN325DC", "course_title": "Cyber Forensics and Cyber Laws", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 66, "hay": "mcn325dc-p3 tamper-evident hardware event logger with sha-256 hash chain cyber forensics and cyber laws mcn325dc forensic readiness in socs needs tamper-evident hardware logs. design an rtl event logger with an sha-256 hash-chained log (using a vetted open core or your own verified implementation), axi-lite interface, and tamper-detection software. event capture sha-256 log memory axi-lite verifier secure soc components. units ii, iii, v: evidence integrity, logging, governance"}, {"id": "MCV325DD-P1", "title": "Fail-Safe Interlock Controller with Formal Verification", "course": "MCV325DD", "course_title": "Industrial Safety and Health", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 67, "hay": "mcv325dd-p1 fail-safe interlock controller with formal verification industrial safety and health mcv325dd safety interlocks must be provably correct. formal methods give strong evidence. design a dual-channel emergency-stop and interlock fsm with voting and watchdog; verify safety properties with symbiyosys. dual-channel inputs voting watchdog formal properties safety design, formal. units i, iv: hazard prevention, accident causation, protective systems"}, {"id": "MCV325DD-P2", "title": "Cleanroom/Lab Hazard Register and Risk Matrix Tool (ISO 45001 Mapping)", "course": "MCV325DD", "course_title": "Industrial Safety and Health", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build next", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 15, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 68, "hay": "mcv325dd-p2 cleanroom/lab hazard register and risk matrix tool (iso 45001 mapping) industrial safety and health mcv325dd semiconductor labs have chemical, electrical and ergonomic hazards that need a structured register. build a web/cli tool for hazard identification, severity-likelihood risk matrix, controls hierarchy and iso 45001 clause mapping, populated with a realistic electronics-lab example. register schema risk scoring controls report systems and process thinking. units ii, iii, v: hazards, exposure limits, ehs management"}, {"id": "MCV325DD-P3", "title": "On-Die Thermal Protection FSM with Throttle for an Accelerator", "course": "MCV325DD", "course_title": "Industrial Safety and Health", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 69, "hay": "mcv325dd-p3 on-die thermal protection fsm with throttle for an accelerator industrial safety and health mcv325dd overtemperature protection is a safety function in chips. its behaviour under sensor fault must be defined. design rtl: sensor interface model, debounce, hysteresis, throttle and shutdown states, and assertions; co-simulate with a thermal rc model; test sensor-fault scenarios. sensor model protection fsm throttle thermal model assertions protection logic. units i, iv: protection layers, system failure causation"}, {"id": "MCV325DE-P1", "title": "GPU Agent-Based Traffic Simulator (Car-Following Models in CUDA)", "course": "MCV325DE", "course_title": "Advanced Technologies for Transportation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 70, "hay": "mcv325de-p1 gpu agent-based traffic simulator (car-following models in cuda) advanced technologies for transportation systems mcv325de traffic microsimulation scales with vehicle count and is data-parallel. implement an intelligent driver model car-following simulation: cpu baseline, cuda with structure-of-arrays, neighbour search via sorting; validate with fundamental diagram. vehicle state soa neighbour search update kernel validation parallel simulation. unit iii: traffic stream models, car-following, simulation"}, {"id": "MCV325DE-P2", "title": "Edge Vision Latency-Budget and Roofline Analysis for Vehicle Detection", "course": "MCV325DE", "course_title": "Advanced Technologies for Transportation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 71, "hay": "mcv325de-p2 edge vision latency-budget and roofline analysis for vehicle detection advanced technologies for transportation systems mcv325de perception pipelines must meet latency; analytical modelling identifies bottlenecks. build a layerwise operations/bytes model of a tiny detection network (compute from layer shapes), apply a roofline model for candidate hardware, and compare predictions to measurements on available hardware. network parser cost model roofline measurement performance modelling. units ii, v: sensors, its applications"}, {"id": "MCV325DE-P3", "title": "Adaptive Signal Controller FSM in RTL with SUMO Co-Simulation", "course": "MCV325DE", "course_title": "Advanced Technologies for Transportation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 72, "hay": "mcv325de-p3 adaptive signal controller fsm in rtl with sumo co-simulation advanced technologies for transportation systems mcv325de real-time adaptive control is a hardware-friendly state machine problem; evaluation needs traffic simulation. implement actuated/webster-style controller in rtl, connect to sumo via traci and a python bridge, evaluate delay against fixed-time control. controller rtl bridge sumo scenario hw/sw co-simulation. units iii, iv, v: signalised intersections, atms"}, {"id": "MEC325DF-P1", "title": "Display Controller RTL: Timing Generator, Framebuffer DMA and Alpha Blender", "course": "MEC325DF", "course_title": "Design & Implementation of Human-Machine Interface", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 73, "hay": "mec325df-p1 display controller rtl: timing generator, framebuffer dma and alpha blender design & implementation of human-machine interface mec325df a display path is a real-time dma problem: underruns show as visual glitches. implement a vga/hdmi-style timing generator, framebuffer reader through axi burst dma, fifo, and alpha blending; simulate with a frame checker and measure underrun conditions. timing gen dma reader fifo blender frame checker dma and real-time streaming. units iii, iv: graphics, rendering, hmi display path"}, {"id": "MEC325DF-P2", "title": "2D Rasteriser and Alpha Compositor on GPU: Frame-Time Budget Study", "course": "MEC325DF", "course_title": "Design & Implementation of Human-Machine Interface", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 17, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 74, "hay": "mec325df-p2 2d rasteriser and alpha compositor on gpu: frame-time budget study design & implementation of human-machine interface mec325df ui frame budgets (16.6 ms at 60 hz) require understanding of gpu compositing cost. write cuda (or opengl compute) kernels for tiled 2d compositing of layers; profile bandwidth; compare with cpu compositor. layer buffers compositor kernel profiler gpu graphics fundamentals. unit iii graphics rendering; unit v ui analytics and profiling"}, {"id": "MEC325DF-P3", "title": "Input-to-Photon Latency Measurement Framework on MCU + RTOS", "course": "MEC325DF", "course_title": "Design & Implementation of Human-Machine Interface", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 75, "hay": "mec325df-p3 input-to-photon latency measurement framework on mcu + rtos design & implementation of human-machine interface mec325df perceived responsiveness depends on end-to-end latency, including interrupts and scheduling. build a framework with hardware timestamping (gpio/timer capture), rtos tasks for touch->render, and a statistical latency analysis with jitter attribution. timestamping rtos pipeline analysis profiling, rtos. units ii, v: automotive hmi usability, testing, profiling"}, {"id": "MEE325DG-P1", "title": "BMS Protection and Cell-Balancing FSM in RTL with Formal Properties", "course": "MEE325DG", "course_title": "Electric Vehicle Technology - II", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 76, "hay": "mee325dg-p1 bms protection and cell-balancing fsm in rtl with formal properties electric vehicle technology - ii mee325dg bms protections are safety-critical state machines suited to formal checks. implement over/under-voltage, over-temperature, passive balancing, and contactor control fsm; formal properties for safety; co-sim with a cell model. monitors protection fsm balancer formal safety fsm design. unit iii: bms objectives, charging/discharging control, cell balancing"}, {"id": "MEE325DG-P2", "title": "J1772 Pilot-Signal and Charging-Session State Machine", "course": "MEE325DG", "course_title": "Electric Vehicle Technology - II", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 77, "hay": "mee325dg-p2 j1772 pilot-signal and charging-session state machine electric vehicle technology - ii mee325dg charging control relies on a pilot pwm and state machine; correct timing is needed. implement an evse-side state machine measuring pilot duty/voltage, with rtos firmware and a protocol simulator testing all states and faults. pilot measure state machine simulator tests protocol firmware. unit v: charging standards (sae j1772), charger architectures"}, {"id": "MEE325DG-P3", "title": "Custom RISC-V Instructions for Clarke/Park Transforms", "course": "MEE325DG", "course_title": "Electric Vehicle Technology - II", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 78, "hay": "mee325dg-p3 custom risc-v instructions for clarke/park transforms electric vehicle technology - ii mee325dg custom instructions can accelerate control kernels; cost versus benefit needs measurement. extend your rv32i core (or ibex/picorv32) with custom instructions for clarke/park transforms and sin/cos lookup, modify the compiler assembly via intrinsics, and measure cycles and area. custom unit decode changes intrinsics benchmark isa extension. unit ii: motor drive control blocks"}, {"id": "MET325DH-P1", "title": "GNSS C/A Acquisition: FFT-Based Parallel Code-Phase Search", "course": "MET325DH", "course_title": "Electronic Navigation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 79, "hay": "met325dh-p1 gnss c/a acquisition: fft-based parallel code-phase search electronic navigation systems met325dh acquisition is a correlation search that maps well to ffts and parallel hardware. implement acquisition in python on public gnss sample data, then optimise with a cuda fft or an optimised cpu fft; compare time and detection reliability. code generator fft correlator detector fft pipelines. unit iii: gnss signals and receivers"}, {"id": "MET325DH-P2", "title": "FMCW Radar Range-Doppler Pipeline: 2D FFT in CUDA and RTL", "course": "MET325DH", "course_title": "Electronic Navigation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 22, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 80, "hay": "met325dh-p2 fmcw radar range-doppler pipeline: 2d fft in cuda and rtl electronic navigation systems met325dh radar processing is fft-heavy and bandwidth-bound; hardware vs gpu trade-offs apply. simulate fmcw chirps, implement range-doppler map with 2d fft in python, cuda, and a streaming rtl fft; compare throughput and resources. signal generator 2d fft cpu cuda rtl fft streaming fft. unit i: radar detection, doppler"}, {"id": "MET325DH-P3", "title": "LiDAR Point-Cloud Voxel Filter and Nearest-Neighbour Search on GPU", "course": "MET325DH", "course_title": "Electronic Navigation Systems", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 81, "hay": "met325dh-p3 lidar point-cloud voxel filter and nearest-neighbour search on gpu electronic navigation systems met325dh point-cloud processing is irregular and memory-bound; spatial structures decide performance. implement voxel-grid downsampling and nearest-neighbour via spatial hashing in cuda; compare with cpu kd-tree; profile memory behaviour. hash grid voxel kernel nn kernel profiler irregular gpu workloads. unit iv: lidar data and processing"}, {"id": "MET325DJ-P1", "title": "Viterbi Decoder RTL for an 802.11p-Style Baseband", "course": "MET325DJ", "course_title": "Vehicular Communication Ecosystem", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 82, "hay": "met325dj-p1 viterbi decoder rtl for an 802.11p-style baseband vehicular communication ecosystem met325dj channel decoders are streaming datapaths with real hardware trade-offs. implement convolutional encoder and viterbi decoder (k=7) with soft decisions in rtl; verify ber against python over awgn. encoder branch metrics acs traceback ber harness communications hardware. unit ii: physical layer considerations"}, {"id": "MET325DJ-P2", "title": "V2X Message Signature Verification Throughput: MCU vs Accelerator", "course": "MET325DJ", "course_title": "Vehicular Communication Ecosystem", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 83, "hay": "met325dj-p2 v2x message signature verification throughput: mcu vs accelerator vehicular communication ecosystem met325dj dense traffic requires verifying many signed messages per second; this is a compute-bound crypto problem. benchmark ecdsa p-256 verification using a vetted library on mcu class and desktop cpu; model message rates at traffic density; evaluate offload to a hypothetical accelerator via a throughput model. benchmark harness traffic model offload model performance analysis. unit iii: data security in vehicular networks, scalability"}, {"id": "MET325DJ-P3", "title": "Automotive Ethernet/CAN Gateway in RTL with Deterministic Latency", "course": "MET325DJ", "course_title": "Vehicular Communication Ecosystem", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 84, "hay": "met325dj-p3 automotive ethernet/can gateway in rtl with deterministic latency vehicular communication ecosystem met325dj gateways translate between networks with latency guarantees. implement frame filter, buffer and priority scheduler gateway in rtl with axi-stream and verify worst-case latency under load. parser filter scheduler latency monitor network hardware. unit iv: in-vehicle networks, bus systems"}, {"id": "MIM325DK-P1", "title": "PERT/CPM Planner with Monte-Carlo Schedule Risk for a Tape-Out-Style Project", "course": "MIM325DK", "course_title": "Essentials of Project Management", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 85, "hay": "mim325dk-p1 pert/cpm planner with monte-carlo schedule risk for a tape-out-style project essentials of project management mim325dk chip projects slip because dependencies are underestimated. build a wbs and activity network for your major project, compute cpm, and run monte-carlo schedule simulation with triangular distributions. wbs cpm engine monte-carlo planning. unit iv: gantt, pert, cpm; unit i wbs"}, {"id": "MIM325DK-P2", "title": "Financial Model for an AI Accelerator Chip (Parametric NRE and Break-Even)", "course": "MIM325DK", "course_title": "Essentials of Project Management", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 16, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 86, "hay": "mim325dk-p2 financial model for an ai accelerator chip (parametric nre and break-even) essentials of project management mim325dk silicon investments depend on nre, unit cost and volume. parametric model: nre items, die cost from yield model, packaging, volume, price, npv, break-even. all numbers are user-entered assumptions with notes. cost inputs npv model sensitivity business reasoning. units ii, iii: capital budgeting, costing, financial modelling"}, {"id": "MIM325DK-P3", "title": "CI/CD for Hardware: Regression, Coverage and PPA Tracking Pipeline", "course": "MIM325DK", "course_title": "Essentials of Project Management", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 87, "hay": "mim325dk-p3 ci/cd for hardware: regression, coverage and ppa tracking pipeline essentials of project management mim325dk hardware teams need continuous regression and ppa tracking to avoid late surprises. set up a ci pipeline (github actions) running verilator/cocotb tests, coverage, linting, synthesis and ppa trend tracking; measure team velocity metrics. test runner coverage synthesis job ppa dashboard engineering process. unit v: agile implementation; unit iv computerized project management"}, {"id": "MIS325DM-P1", "title": "Interactive Design-Space Explorer for Accelerator Results with Usability Testing", "course": "MIS325DM", "course_title": "User Interface & User Experience", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 88, "hay": "mis325dm-p1 interactive design-space explorer for accelerator results with usability testing user interface & user experience mis325dm engineers must compare hundreds of design points; poor ui hides insight. build a static web app that explores a ppa design-space csv (filter, pareto, scatter), test with 5 users, iterate. data loader charts filters usability study ui engineering. units i, ii, iii: patterns, prototyping, testing"}, {"id": "MIS325DM-P2", "title": "Timing-Report and Waveform Viewer for Web", "course": "MIS325DM", "course_title": "User Interface & User Experience", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 89, "hay": "mis325dm-p2 timing-report and waveform viewer for web user interface & user experience mis325dm debugging needs fast visual exploration of vcd waveforms and sta paths. create a browser viewer for vcd subsets and opensta path reports, with search, zoom, and keyboard accessibility. vcd parser renderer path view tooling. units iv, v: findability, accessibility, credibility"}, {"id": "MIS325DM-P3", "title": "Register-Map Generator: YAML to RTL, C Headers, Docs and Web Explorer", "course": "MIS325DM", "course_title": "User Interface & User Experience", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 90, "hay": "mis325dm-p3 register-map generator: yaml to rtl, c headers, docs and web explorer user interface & user experience mis325dm register maps drift between rtl, drivers and docs. a single source of truth prevents bugs. define yaml csr spec; generate axi-lite register rtl, c header, markdown docs, and an interactive web explorer; verify with generated tests. prior tools exist (for example peakrdl); this is a learning implementation. spec schema rtl gen c gen docs/web tests hw/sw interface. units i, iii: patterns and usefulness; hw/sw interface"}, {"id": "MMA325DN-P1", "title": "Quantisation Error Analysis: SQNR Theory vs Measurement and Optimal Clipping", "course": "MMA325DN", "course_title": "Mathematical Methods for Data Science", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 91, "hay": "mma325dn-p1 quantisation error analysis: sqnr theory vs measurement and optimal clipping mathematical methods for data science mma325dn quantisation scale choice is an optimisation problem; theory predicts sqnr. derive sqnr for uniform quantisers; measure on real weight/activation distributions; find clipping thresholds by mse minimisation and kl-style search; compare. distribution fit quantiser optimiser evaluation numerical analysis. unit i estimation; unit ii/iii optimisation"}, {"id": "MMA325DN-P2", "title": "Surrogate-Model Optimisation of Accelerator PPA from Synthesis Data", "course": "MMA325DN", "course_title": "Mathematical Methods for Data Science", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 92, "hay": "mma325dn-p2 surrogate-model optimisation of accelerator ppa from synthesis data mathematical methods for data science mma325dn running synthesis for every design point is slow. surrogates can guide the search. generate ppa data by synthesising parameterised arrays, train regression surrogates, and use bayesian optimisation to find pareto designs; compare with random search. data generation surrogates bo comparison ml for design. units ii, iii, v: optimisation, regression"}, {"id": "MMA325DN-P3", "title": "Reduced-Precision Number Formats: bfloat16, FP8 and Block Floating Point Emulation", "course": "MMA325DN", "course_title": "Mathematical Methods for Data Science", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 93, "hay": "mma325dn-p3 reduced-precision number formats: bfloat16, fp8 and block floating point emulation mathematical methods for data science mma325dn number format choice determines accelerator area and accuracy. emulation allows study without silicon. emulate int8, bfloat16, fp8 (e4m3/e5m2 as in micikevicius et al., 2022), and block floating point for matmul, softmax and layernorm of a small transformer; relate accuracy to estimated mac area. format emulators layer tests area model numerics. units iii, iv, v: optimisation, ann, loss functions"}, {"id": "MME325DO-P1", "title": "TinyML Vibration Anomaly Detector on MCU", "course": "MME325DO", "course_title": "Industry 4.0: The Smart Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 94, "hay": "mme325do-p1 tinyml vibration anomaly detector on mcu industry 4.0: the smart manufacturing mme325do predictive maintenance runs at the edge with tight memory. train an autoencoder/classifier on public vibration datasets, quantise to int8, deploy with c runtime, measure memory and latency. feature extraction model runtime evaluation tinyml. unit iii: predictive maintenance, data analytics"}, {"id": "MME325DO-P2", "title": "Edge Predictive-Maintenance SoC Reference Architecture and HW/SW Partition Study", "course": "MME325DO", "course_title": "Industry 4.0: The Smart Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 95, "hay": "mme325do-p2 edge predictive-maintenance soc reference architecture and hw/sw partition study industry 4.0: the smart manufacturing mme325do choosing what runs on cpu vs accelerator drives cost and power. define workload (fft, features, small nn), profile on cpu model, evaluate partition options with analytical latency/energy models, and produce an architecture document. workload profiler partition model architecture doc hw/sw partitioning. units ii, iii: edge computing, iiot, analytics"}, {"id": "MME325DO-P3", "title": "ASCON-128 vs AES-128 Lightweight Crypto in RTL: Area and Energy", "course": "MME325DO", "course_title": "Industry 4.0: The Smart Manufacturing", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 96, "hay": "mme325do-p3 ascon-128 vs aes-128 lightweight crypto in rtl: area and energy industry 4.0: the smart manufacturing mme325do iiot nodes need lightweight authenticated encryption; hardware cost comparison informs choice. implement ascon-128 (nist lightweight standard selection) and aes-128 in rtl, verify with known-answer tests, synthesise and compare area/throughput/energy. ascon core aes core kats synthesis crypto hardware. unit v: industrial iot security"}, {"id": "MME325DQ-P1", "title": "Sensor-to-Cloud Pipeline: RTOS Drivers, MQTT and Reliability Measurement", "course": "MME325DQ", "course_title": "Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT)", "sem": 2, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 97, "hay": "mme325dq-p1 sensor-to-cloud pipeline: rtos drivers, mqtt and reliability measurement industrial internet of things (iiot) mme325dq iiot reliability depends on drivers, buffering and network handling. build sensor drivers on rtos, local buffering, mqtt publish with qos, simulate packet loss and measure data loss. drivers buffer mqtt client fault injector embedded networking. units ii, iv: sensor interfacing, networking"}, {"id": "MME325DQ-P2", "title": "FPGA Multi-Sensor DAQ with Precise Timestamping", "course": "MME325DQ", "course_title": "Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT)", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 98, "hay": "mme325dq-p2 fpga multi-sensor daq with precise timestamping industrial internet of things (iiot) mme325dq time alignment across sensors needs hardware timestamping. implement spi adc readers, timestamp counter, fifos and an axi-stream/uart output in rtl; measure sample jitter in simulation. spi master timestamp fifo output interface hardware. units ii, iv: sensor interfacing, fieldbus, data capture"}, {"id": "MME325DQ-P3", "title": "Streaming Anomaly Detector (Online z-score/k-means) in RTL", "course": "MME325DQ", "course_title": "Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 99, "hay": "mme325dq-p3 streaming anomaly detector (online z-score/k-means) in rtl industrial internet of things (iiot) mme325dq streaming analytics at the edge needs fixed-point, constant-memory algorithms. implement online mean/variance and k-means update in fixed-point rtl; verify against python; evaluate detection quality on a public sensor dataset. stats unit k-means unit interface reference streaming ml hardware. unit v: clustering, prediction models, error metrics"}, {"id": "MIM426RT-P1", "title": "Systematic Literature Map: INT8/INT4 Systolic and Dataflow Accelerators", "course": "MIM426RT", "course_title": "Research Methodology (NPTEL)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 100, "hay": "mim426rt-p1 systematic literature map: int8/int4 systolic and dataflow accelerators research methodology (nptel) mim426rt without a structured survey, projects repeat known work. build a protocol-driven literature map (search strings, inclusion rules, tagged bibtex database) of at least 30 papers, classify by dataflow, precision, memory, and evaluation methodology, and identify genuine gaps. verify every citation exists before including it. protocol search screening classification gap analysis research skills. weeks 1-2: literature survey; week 4: writing"}, {"id": "MIM426RT-P2", "title": "Design of Experiments for EDA Flow Parameters (Factorial/Taguchi with ANOVA)", "course": "MIM426RT", "course_title": "Research Methodology (NPTEL)", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 101, "hay": "mim426rt-p2 design of experiments for eda flow parameters (factorial/taguchi with anova) research methodology (nptel) mim426rt tuning eda flows by trial and error wastes time; doe gives structured evidence. choose 5 flow parameters, run a fractional factorial or taguchi design on openroad across designs and seeds, analyse main effects and interactions with anova. design matrix runner anova confirmation runs experimental method. week 3 data analysis; week 6 design of experiments"}, {"id": "MIM426RT-P3", "title": "Artifact-Style Replication of a Published Accelerator Energy Model + IP/Licensing Review", "course": "MIM426RT", "course_title": "Research Methodology (NPTEL)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 102, "hay": "mim426rt-p3 artifact-style replication of a published accelerator energy model + ip/licensing review research methodology (nptel) mim426rt reproducibility and licensing are part of professional research. replicate an energy/latency analysis using timeloop-style analytical modelling (parashar et al., ispass 2019) on a layer set; write artifact-evaluation style documentation; review licences (apache-2.0, cern-ohl, gpl) of tools you reuse. replication artifact docs licence review reproducibility. weeks 5-7: ethics, creativity, intellectual property"}, {"id": "MVE427SL-P1", "title": "Common-Centroid Current Mirror and Differential Pair Layout with Mismatch Study", "course": "MVE427SL", "course_title": "Skill Lab (Layout Design Lab)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 103, "hay": "mve427sl-p1 common-centroid current mirror and differential pair layout with mismatch study skill lab (layout design lab) mve427sl matching quality depends on layout technique, not only schematic. lay out a current mirror and diff pair with simple vs common-centroid/interdigitated styles, extract parasitics, and run monte-carlo mismatch simulations. schematic layouts (2 styles) pex mc sim analog layout. modules 1-3, 5"}, {"id": "MVE427SL-P2", "title": "Mini Standard-Cell Library: Layout, Characterisation to Liberty and OpenROAD Use", "course": "MVE427SL", "course_title": "Skill Lab (Layout Design Lab)", "sem": 2, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-9", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 104, "hay": "mve427sl-p2 mini standard-cell library: layout, characterisation to liberty and openroad use skill lab (layout design lab) mve427sl standard cell libraries tie layout to timing models. building one clarifies the stack. lay out inv, nand2, nor2, aoi21 and a dff; script ngspice characterisation to liberty tables, generate lef; run a small design through openroad with your cells. layouts characterisation scripts liberty lef flow test library flow. modules 6-9: inv, nand, aoi/oai, library development"}, {"id": "MVE427SL-P3", "title": "Ring-Oscillator Process Monitor Array: Layout, Simulation and Variation Mapping", "course": "MVE427SL", "course_title": "Skill Lab (Layout Design Lab)", "sem": 2, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-2.html", "ord": 105, "hay": "mve427sl-p3 ring-oscillator process monitor array: layout, simulation and variation mapping skill lab (layout design lab) mve427sl on-die monitors reveal process variation; layout parasitics shift frequency. design ring oscillators of varied types/loads, lay out an array, compare pre/post-layout frequency, and use monte-carlo to relate frequency to variation; ties to design-thinking study. rings layout extraction mc analysis variation monitors. module 4: vco pre/post-layout"}, {"id": "MVE331TA-P1", "title": "Partitioning and Simulated-Annealing Floorplanner on Public Benchmarks", "course": "MVE331TA", "course_title": "Algorithms for VLSI Design Automation", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 106, "hay": "mve331ta-p1 partitioning and simulated-annealing floorplanner on public benchmarks algorithms for vlsi design automation mve331ta understanding sa and group migration algorithms clarifies how tools trade runtime and quality. implement kl/fm partitioning and an sa floorplanner (sequence-pair or b*-tree) on mcnc/gsrc-style benchmarks; compare to random and report area and wirelength. benchmark parser fm sa floorplanner metrics eda algorithms. unit ii partitioning (kl/fm, sa); unit iii floorplanning"}, {"id": "MVE331TA-P2", "title": "Maze and Steiner-Based Global Router Compared with OpenROAD", "course": "MVE331TA", "course_title": "Algorithms for VLSI Design Automation", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 107, "hay": "mve331ta-p2 maze and steiner-based global router compared with openroad algorithms for vlsi design automation mve331ta routing congestion limits designs; algorithms decide overflow. implement a grid global router (lee/a* with rip-up and reroute, steiner tree decomposition) and evaluate overflow and wirelength against openroad's router on small benchmarks. grid model maze router rrr comparison routing algorithms. unit iv global routing: maze, line probe, steiner tree"}, {"id": "MVE331TA-P3", "title": "GPU-Accelerated Analytic Placement Kernels (HPWL and Density) in CUDA", "course": "MVE331TA", "course_title": "Algorithms for VLSI Design Automation", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-12", "w0": 8, "total": 27, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 108, "hay": "mve331ta-p3 gpu-accelerated analytic placement kernels (hpwl and density) in cuda algorithms for vlsi design automation mve331ta placement is a large optimisation. gpu acceleration is established (for example dreamplace, lin et al., dac 2019), so the contribution here is a learning implementation and kernel study. implement a simple analytic placer on cpu (weighted-average wirelength plus density), then cuda kernels for wirelength gradient and density smoothing; profile and measure speedup and solution quality. cpu placer wa wirelength kernel density kernel optimiser profiler gpu kernels for irregular problems, eda. unit iii placement; parallel-algorithm view of units ii and iv"}, {"id": "MVE232E1-P1", "title": "Stuck-At Fault Simulator and PODEM ATPG on ISCAS Circuits", "course": "MVE232E1", "course_title": "Digital VLSI Testing", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 109, "hay": "mve232e1-p1 stuck-at fault simulator and podem atpg on iscas circuits digital vlsi testing mve232e1 understanding test generation internals clarifies coverage. implement a parallel-pattern fault simulator and podem for combinational iscas circuits; report coverage vs patterns. netlist parser fault list fault sim podem atpg. weeks 4-6: fault simulation, atpg"}, {"id": "MVE232E1-P2", "title": "Scan, LFSR/MISR BIST Insertion and Fault Coverage for a MAC Array", "course": "MVE232E1", "course_title": "Digital VLSI Testing", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 110, "hay": "mve232e1-p2 scan, lfsr/misr bist insertion and fault coverage for a mac array digital vlsi testing mve232e1 testing accelerators needs scan and bist schemes with acceptable overhead. add scan chains (using an open dft flow or own scripts) and logic bist (lfsr/misr) to a mac array; evaluate fault coverage vs pattern count and area overhead. scan insertion lfsr misr fault sim dft. weeks 2-3, 6-7: scan design, bist"}, {"id": "MVE232E1-P3", "title": "March-Test Memory BIST for Accelerator Scratchpad with Fault Injection", "course": "MVE232E1", "course_title": "Digital VLSI Testing", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 111, "hay": "mve232e1-p3 march-test memory bist for accelerator scratchpad with fault injection digital vlsi testing mve232e1 scratchpads dominate accelerator area; memory defects dominate yield. implement a march c- bist controller in rtl for a scratchpad, with fault models (saf, tf, coupling) injected in simulation; evaluate coverage and overhead; explore testing pe-array structures using column-wise patterns. memory model with faults march fsm coverage analysis memory test. weeks 10-11: memory testing, mbist"}, {"id": "MVE232E2-P1", "title": "HLS GEMM and FIR Kernels with Pragma Exploration", "course": "MVE232E2", "course_title": "C Based - VLSI Design", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 112, "hay": "mve232e2-p1 hls gemm and fir kernels with pragma exploration c based - vlsi design mve232e2 hls performance depends on code structure and directives. write c++ kernels for fir and gemm, explore unrolling, pipelining and array partitioning in an hls tool, and tabulate latency/ii/resources. c++ kernels pragma sweep reports hls. weeks 3-7: hls flow, scheduling, hardware-efficient c"}, {"id": "MVE232E2-P2", "title": "HLS vs Hand-Written RTL for a Conv Engine: PPA and Productivity", "course": "MVE232E2", "course_title": "C Based - VLSI Design", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 113, "hay": "mve232e2-p2 hls vs hand-written rtl for a conv engine: ppa and productivity c based - vlsi design mve232e2 hls promises productivity; the cost in ppa must be measured. implement the same conv engine in hls and rtl, compare resources, fmax, latency and development effort (hours, lines). hls design rtl design comparison design methodology. weeks 5-9: binding, efficient c coding, verification"}, {"id": "MVE232E2-P3", "title": "Auto-Tuned HLS Design-Space Exploration with C/RTL Equivalence Checking", "course": "MVE232E2", "course_title": "C Based - VLSI Design", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 114, "hay": "mve232e2-p3 auto-tuned hls design-space exploration with c/rtl equivalence checking c based - vlsi design mve232e2 manual hls tuning is slow; search plus verification can automate it. build a search harness (bayesian or evolutionary) over pragma configurations with automatic c/rtl co-simulation to guard correctness; compare to random. config generator runner co-sim optimiser dse automation. weeks 9, 12: verification of hls, recent advances"}, {"id": "MVE232E3-P1", "title": "Elmore Delay Calculator for RC Trees Verified against ngspice", "course": "MVE232E3", "course_title": "VLSI Interconnects", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Beginner", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-4", "w0": 3, "total": 17, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 115, "hay": "mve232e3-p1 elmore delay calculator for rc trees verified against ngspice vlsi interconnects mve232e3 elmore delay is the basis of many timing estimates. implement elmore and second-moment delay for rc trees in python; compare with ngspice transient for distributed lines; document accuracy limits. rc tree parser elmore spice compare interconnect modelling. weeks 1-2: elmore delay, rc trees"}, {"id": "MVE232E3-P2", "title": "Coupled-Line Crosstalk and Repeater Insertion Study with Extracted Parasitics", "course": "MVE232E3", "course_title": "VLSI Interconnects", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 116, "hay": "mve232e3-p2 coupled-line crosstalk and repeater insertion study with extracted parasitics vlsi interconnects mve232e3 spacing, shielding and repeaters trade delay, noise and area. extract parasitics from magic layouts of coupled lines; simulate delay/noise vs spacing and shielding; optimise repeaters analytically and by simulation. layouts extraction spice repeater optimiser signal integrity. weeks 6-8: crosstalk mitigation, coupled interconnects, capacitance extraction"}, {"id": "MVE232E3-P3", "title": "Wire-Energy-Aware Dataflow Study: Energy per Bit-mm in Systolic Arrays", "course": "MVE232E3", "course_title": "VLSI Interconnects", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 23, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 117, "hay": "mve232e3-p3 wire-energy-aware dataflow study: energy per bit-mm in systolic arrays vlsi interconnects mve232e3 data movement energy often dominates compute (see horowitz, isscc 2014). how does array size interact with wire energy? build a wire energy and delay model from extracted rc, apply it to systolic dataflows of various sizes, and identify where wire costs make larger arrays inefficient. wire model dataflow model scaling study physical-architecture thinking. weeks 1-5: scaling, power dissipation of interconnects"}, {"id": "MVE232E4-P1", "title": "Sensor Interface and Calibration Firmware (Thermistor/MEMS) with Accuracy Study", "course": "MVE232E4", "course_title": "Sensor Technologies: Physics, Fabrication and Circuits", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "3-5", "w0": 3, "total": 16, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 118, "hay": "mve232e4-p1 sensor interface and calibration firmware (thermistor/mems) with accuracy study sensor technologies: physics, fabrication and circuits mve232e4 raw adc counts need calibration and filtering. implement adc reading, linearisation (steinhart-hart or polynomial), noise filtering and temperature compensation on an mcu; evaluate accuracy. adc driver calibration filter sensor firmware. weeks 1-2, 8: basics, circuits"}, {"id": "MVE232E4-P2", "title": "On-Sensor TinyML: Accelerometer Activity Classifier with Energy per Inference", "course": "MVE232E4", "course_title": "Sensor Technologies: Physics, Fabrication and Circuits", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 119, "hay": "mve232e4-p2 on-sensor tinyml: accelerometer activity classifier with energy per inference sensor technologies: physics, fabrication and circuits mve232e4 always-on sensing needs sub-mw inference. train a small classifier on public imu datasets, quantise, implement in c on mcu or risc-v softcore model, estimate energy per inference. features model c inference energy model edge ml. weeks 3-5, 8: sensor physics and systems"}, {"id": "MVE232E4-P3", "title": "Capacitive Readout Chain: Switched-Capacitor Front End + CIC Filter RTL", "course": "MVE232E4", "course_title": "Sensor Technologies: Physics, Fabrication and Circuits", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 120, "hay": "mve232e4-p3 capacitive readout chain: switched-capacitor front end + cic filter rtl sensor technologies: physics, fabrication and circuits mve232e4 readout noise vs power defines sensor quality. model a capacitive sensor front-end in ngspice, digitise with a behavioural sigma-delta, implement decimation (cic) in rtl, and analyse noise vs power. front-end modulator model cic rtl noise analysis mixed-signal. weeks 6-8: fabrication/characterisation, circuits"}, {"id": "MVE433P-P1", "title": "INT8 Systolic GEMM Accelerator: AXI-Lite CSRs, DMA, Verified, with PPA", "course": "MVE433P", "course_title": "Minor Project", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build first", "wk": "12-16", "w0": 12, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 121, "hay": "mve433p-p1 int8 systolic gemm accelerator: axi-lite csrs, dma, verified, with ppa minor project mve433p a complete accelerator block with a software-visible interface is the portfolio centrepiece; the gap is rigorous verification and ppa evidence. weight-stationary array with scratchpads, axi-lite csrs, axi-stream or dma data path, host driver, golden-model verification, coverage and openlane ppa. evaluate against a cpu baseline and the gemm scheduler. csr block dma scratchpads systolic array controller driver verification accelerator design, verification, pd. guideline: literature survey, gap analysis, in-house implementation"}, {"id": "MVE433P-P2", "title": "GEMM Optimisation Ladder on GPU with Roofline: Naive to Tensor-Core-Aware", "course": "MVE433P", "course_title": "Minor Project", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "10-14", "w0": 10, "total": 26, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 122, "hay": "mve433p-p2 gemm optimisation ladder on gpu with roofline: naive to tensor-core-aware minor project mve433p gemm is the core workload; optimisation steps and profiler evidence show gpu architecture understanding. implement naive, coalesced, shared-memory tiled, register-blocked and (where supported) tensor-core wmma kernels; compare with cublas; roofline and counter analysis. kernels harness profiling roofline cuda optimisation. guideline: in-house implementation and analysis"}, {"id": "MVE433P-P3", "title": "RV32 Core + Custom MAC Instruction + Small Cache: Measured CPI and Energy Proxy", "course": "MVE433P", "course_title": "Minor Project", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "10-14", "w0": 10, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 123, "hay": "mve433p-p3 rv32 core + custom mac instruction + small cache: measured cpi and energy proxy minor project mve433p custom instructions and cache design show soc-level architecture skills. build on the rv32i core (mve214a4): add a dot-product/mac instruction, a direct-mapped i/d cache, and compiler intrinsics; run kernels and coremark; measure cpi, area and speedup. core custom unit caches intrinsics benchmarks cpu architecture. guideline: in-house implementation"}, {"id": "MVE434N-P1", "title": "Verification-Focused Internship Plan: Coverage Closure on an Open IP Block", "course": "MVE434N", "course_title": "Internship", "sem": 3, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "6", "w0": 6, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 124, "hay": "mve434n-p1 verification-focused internship plan: coverage closure on an open ip block internship mve434n verification hiring is large; a concrete coverage and bug-finding record is persuasive. pick an open ip block (for example from a mainstream open-source risc-v or peripheral repository), build or extend a testbench, close defined coverage goals, file reproducible issues. if the host is a company, adapt to their ip under nda rules. plan testbench coverage issue log verification practice. internship guideline: field-related industry/research work"}, {"id": "MVE434N-P2", "title": "PPA-Improvement Internship Plan on an Open Design", "course": "MVE434N", "course_title": "Internship", "sem": 3, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6", "w0": 6, "total": 24, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 125, "hay": "mve434n-p2 ppa-improvement internship plan on an open design internship mve434n physical-design roles value measured ppa improvements with discipline. take an open design through a documented flow, identify bottlenecks, apply targeted fixes, and report before/after ppa; with company tools if host provides. baseline analysis changes report physical design. internship guideline"}, {"id": "MVE434N-P3", "title": "GPU/Firmware Performance-Engineering Internship Plan", "course": "MVE434N", "course_title": "Internship", "sem": 3, "track": "B", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": true, "page": "semester-3.html", "ord": 126, "hay": "mve434n-p3 gpu/firmware performance-engineering internship plan internship mve434n performance and bring-up skills are a distinct career path. profile a workload (cuda, cpu or firmware), identify bottlenecks, optimise, and document with before/after measurements; or perform a firmware bring-up task with documented tests. baseline profile optimise report performance engineering. internship guideline"}, {"id": "MVE341F1-P1", "title": "One-Command Reproducible RTL-to-GDS Flow with Sign-Off Checklist", "course": "MVE341F1", "course_title": "VLSI Design Flow: RTL to GDS", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 127, "hay": "mve341f1-p1 one-command reproducible rtl-to-gds flow with sign-off checklist vlsi design flow: rtl to gds mve341f1 reproducibility is what makes ppa claims credible. wrap the open flow in a makefile or workflow with pinned tool versions, containerisation, automated sign-off checks (drc, lvs, sta, lec) and archived reports for two designs. container flow wrapper checks reports flow engineering. weeks 2-12: complete flow"}, {"id": "MVE341F1-P2", "title": "Tape-Out Readiness Dashboard and Regression Across Designs", "course": "MVE341F1", "course_title": "VLSI Design Flow: RTL to GDS", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build next", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 19, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 128, "hay": "mve341f1-p2 tape-out readiness dashboard and regression across designs vlsi design flow: rtl to gds mve341f1 teams track dozens of metrics across blocks; automation reveals regressions. collect metrics from multiple designs and runs, build a dashboard with thresholds, trends, and failure drill-down. collectors db dashboard tooling. weeks 7-12: sta, constraints, physical verification"}, {"id": "MVE341F1-P3", "title": "Flow Hyperparameter Tuning with Bayesian Optimisation vs DoE", "course": "MVE341F1", "course_title": "VLSI Design Flow: RTL to GDS", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 25, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 129, "hay": "mve341f1-p3 flow hyperparameter tuning with bayesian optimisation vs doe vlsi design flow: rtl to gds mve341f1 flow parameters strongly affect ppa; openroad already offers an autotuner, so the contribution is a controlled comparison. compare random search, doe (from mim426rt), openroad autotuner and your bo on multiple designs with seeds; report results with statistics. search space runner optimisers stats eda optimisation. weeks 4-12: optimisation parameters across flow"}, {"id": "MVE341F2-P1", "title": "Type-II Charge-Pump PLL Behavioural Model and Loop-Filter Design", "course": "MVE341F2", "course_title": "Phase-Locked Loops", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 18, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 130, "hay": "mve341f2-p1 type-ii charge-pump pll behavioural model and loop-filter design phase-locked loops mve341f2 loop filter design sets bandwidth, phase margin and lock time. build a python/verilog-ams-free behavioural model and a discrete-time simulation; design loop filter for phase margin; compare with simulation lock time. model loop filter design simulation pll design. weeks 1-5: pll analysis, design procedure"}, {"id": "MVE341F2-P2", "title": "All-Digital PLL in RTL (TDC, Loop Filter, DCO Model)", "course": "MVE341F2", "course_title": "Phase-Locked Loops", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "6-8", "w0": 6, "total": 21, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 131, "hay": "mve341f2-p2 all-digital pll in rtl (tdc, loop filter, dco model) phase-locked loops mve341f2 digital plls are synthesizable and scalable. implement tdc, digital loop filter, dco behavioural model; verify lock, jitter and tracking via simulation. tdc filter dco model jitter analysis digital clocking. weeks 11-12: digital plls"}, {"id": "MVE341F2-P3", "title": "PLL Jitter Budget and Its Effect on MAC-Array Fmax", "course": "MVE341F2", "course_title": "Phase-Locked Loops", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "7-9", "w0": 7, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 132, "hay": "mve341f2-p3 pll jitter budget and its effect on mac-array fmax phase-locked loops mve341f2 jitter consumes timing margin; the question is how much frequency is lost. characterise ring-oscillator jitter in spice, convert to uncertainty, apply in sta constraints for the mac array, and quantify fmax loss vs jitter. jitter extraction sta constraint link fmax study cross-domain analysis. weeks 5-9: noise analysis, ring oscillators, phase noise"}, {"id": "MVE341F3-P1", "title": "SAR ADC Behavioural Model with Capacitor Mismatch and ENOB Analysis", "course": "MVE341F3", "course_title": "VLSI Data Conversion Circuits", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "4-6", "w0": 4, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 133, "hay": "mve341f3-p1 sar adc behavioural model with capacitor mismatch and enob analysis vlsi data conversion circuits mve341f3 enob depends on mismatch, noise and comparator offset. model a sar adc in python with capacitor mismatch and comparator noise, compute inl/dnl/enob, and implement the sar logic in rtl. behavioural model metrics sar rtl data converters. weeks 3-6: metrics, sar converters"}, {"id": "MVE341F3-P2", "title": "Sigma-Delta Modulator Study with CIC Decimator in RTL", "course": "MVE341F3", "course_title": "VLSI Data Conversion Circuits", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 20, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 134, "hay": "mve341f3-p2 sigma-delta modulator study with cic decimator in rtl vlsi data conversion circuits mve341f3 oversampling trades speed for resolution; noise shaping needs decimation hardware. simulate first/second-order modulators, compute snr vs osr, implement cic decimation in rtl and validate end-to-end. modulator model cic snr analysis mixed-signal dsp. weeks 2-5: sampling, oversampling converters"}, {"id": "MVE341F3-P3", "title": "ADC Resolution vs Accuracy and Energy in Compute-in-Memory Readout (System Study)", "course": "MVE341F3", "course_title": "VLSI Data Conversion Circuits", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "8-10", "w0": 8, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 135, "hay": "mve341f3-p3 adc resolution vs accuracy and energy in compute-in-memory readout (system study) vlsi data conversion circuits mve341f3 analog compute-in-memory moves cost to adcs. the question is how adc bits trade accuracy, energy and throughput. model crossbar column sums with noise and variation, quantise with adc models of various resolutions, evaluate network accuracy and adc energy using published figure-of-merit assumptions (for example murmann's adc survey as a labelled source); simulation-only. crossbar model adc model accuracy evaluation energy model analog-digital system analysis. weeks 1-7: adc metrics and architectures applied to analog dot-product readout"}, {"id": "MVE341F4-P1", "title": "Cache and MESI Coherence Simulator with Litmus Tests", "course": "MVE341F4", "course_title": "Multi-Core Computer Architecture", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Intermediate", "label": "Build first", "wk": "5-7", "w0": 5, "total": 22, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 136, "hay": "mve341f4-p1 cache and mesi coherence simulator with litmus tests multi-core computer architecture mve341f4 coherence bugs are subtle; a simulator with litmus tests builds protocol understanding. implement a multicore cache hierarchy with mesi in python/c++, run synthetic and trace workloads, verify invariants and litmus tests. cache model mesi fsm bus/directory invariant checker coherence. weeks 6-8: caches, optimisations, coherence"}, {"id": "MVE341F4-P2", "title": "Wormhole Mesh NoC Router RTL with Virtual Channels and Traffic Generators", "course": "MVE341F4", "course_title": "Multi-Core Computer Architecture", "sem": 4, "track": "C", "diff": "Advanced", "label": "Build next", "wk": "7-10", "w0": 7, "total": 28, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 137, "hay": "mve341f4-p2 wormhole mesh noc router rtl with virtual channels and traffic generators multi-core computer architecture mve341f4 noc routers determine on-chip bandwidth and latency in multi-core and accelerator socs. implement a 5-port wormhole router (xy routing, credit flow control, virtual channels), build a 4x4 mesh, drive with traffic patterns, verify no deadlock, and measure latency-throughput curves vs the algorithm-level study from mdc324c2. input buffers routing vc allocator switch allocator traffic generators noc hardware. weeks 10-12: tiled multicore and noc, energy efficiency, qos"}, {"id": "MVE341F4-P3", "title": "Mini SIMT GPU from Scratch: ISA, Warp Scheduler, Divergence Stack and Kernels", "course": "MVE341F4", "course_title": "Multi-Core Computer Architecture", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "12-18", "w0": 12, "total": 28, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 138, "hay": "mve341f4-p3 mini simt gpu from scratch: isa, warp scheduler, divergence stack and kernels multi-core computer architecture mve341f4 understanding gpus at microarchitecture level is rare. open projects exist (for example vortex, tine et al., micro 2021), so the value is the learning and the measured study, not novelty. design a small simt core: isa, assembler, warps, scoreboard, divergence handling, shared memory, simple memory coalescing; run vector-add, reduction and tiled matmul kernels; add performance counters and compare scaling trends qualitatively with cuda experiments from mve433p. isa + assembler fetch/decode warp scheduler lanes/alu divergence stack memory unit counters gpu microarchitecture. week 5: superscalar and gpu architectures; weeks 1-4 pipelines"}, {"id": "MVE442P-P1", "title": "Quantised Inference SoC: RISC-V Host + INT8 Systolic Engine + DMA, FPGA and PPA", "course": "MVE442P", "course_title": "Major Project", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Build first", "wk": "18", "w0": 18, "total": 27, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 139, "hay": "mve442p-p1 quantised inference soc: risc-v host + int8 systolic engine + dma, fpga and ppa major project mve442p integration is the hard part: host, driver, dma, accelerator and memory must work together and be measured against baselines. integrate your rv32 core, the minor-project accelerator, dma and scratchpads over axi; software stack with driver and a compiler-like layer mapping a small cnn or transformer block; end-to-end inference, measured against cpu and gpu baselines. risc-v host axi interconnect dma accelerator driver model mapper benchmarks soc integration, hw/sw co-design. guideline: gap analysis, individual implementation, publication"}, {"id": "MVE442P-P2", "title": "Mixed-Precision Sparse Dataflow Accelerator with Analytical Model Validated Against RTL", "course": "MVE442P", "course_title": "Major Project", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Build next", "wk": "18", "w0": 18, "total": 28, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 140, "hay": "mve442p-p2 mixed-precision sparse dataflow accelerator with analytical model validated against rtl major project mve442p architecture exploration needs fast models validated against rtl; the claim is model accuracy and design guidance. implement int8/int4 and structured sparsity support in rtl; build a timeloop-style analytical model; validate cycle counts and energy trends vs rtl; explore dataflows; related published work is extensive (for example eyeriss, eie, scnn, timeloop) so define your gap precisely. precision-scalable pe sparse controller analytical model rtl validation dse architecture research. guideline: research gap analysis, publication"}, {"id": "MVE442P-P3", "title": "Open SIMT GPU with CUDA-Like Toolchain: Assembler, Runtime and Performance Counters", "course": "MVE442P", "course_title": "Major Project", "sem": 4, "track": "A", "diff": "Research-grade", "label": "Stretch", "wk": "18", "w0": 18, "total": 26, "cuda": false, "page": "semester-4.html", "ord": 141, "hay": "mve442p-p3 open simt gpu with cuda-like toolchain: assembler, runtime and performance counters major project mve442p a vertically integrated mini-gpu stack shows microarchitecture, compiler and runtime/firmware understanding. extend the simt core (mve341f4) with a small c-subset-to-isa compiler front-end or ptx-like assembler, a host runtime/driver (launch, memory copy, completion), and counters; run gemm and reduction kernels and analyse scaling; compare qualitatively with cuda experiments. simt core compiler runtime/driver host interface counters kernels gpu systems stack. guideline: individual implementation, publication"}]};